  "security_implications": "The register interface top (hmac_reg_top) uses assertions to enforce proper TL-UL handshake and address one-hot property, which are critical for preventing multi‑register writes or reads. A violation of the one‑hot0 property on addr_hit could cause simultaneous access to multiple registers, including those holding keys, digests, or masks, leading to information leakage or unauthorized configuration changes. The commented‑out parity assumption (reqParity) removes request‑channel integrity checking; corrupted write data, addresses, or byte enables could propagate to security‑sensitive registers undetected. reg_busy tied to 0 implies no back‑pressure, so any violation of the single‑cycle pulse requirement on reg_we or reg_re may result in unintended multiple operations.",
  "evidence_refs": [
